      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure this is a good idea, since power signals can be very latency-sensitive and it\u0027s difficult to predict what workq latency will be like, especially since this update gets queued behind anything else that might be waiting and could itself yield to another thread if this isn\u0027t put at the head of the queue (`CONFIG_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_NO_YIELD`).\n\nThis seems like one of those situations where we really do need to do processing in an ISR (and that\u0027s okay because it never blocks), but it might be okay to drop it off the ISR context if we have evidence that the timing is okay for what any AP might need.",
      "revId": "186b7a1be94ea0d94aa586bd3eef318e8c3a8d55",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8d97f220_daf26a64",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-13T23:53:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So one concern was the depth of the ISR stack - when power_signal_interrupt gets called, it may be invoked from any of the GPIOs, virtual wire, ADC comparators, so there is already potentially a significant depth at that point, and power_update_signals has to walk all the signals and update them, so each interrupt has a fair cost at that point. But maybe power signals don\u0027t happen all that frequently.\nPotentially some of the funny crashes we had may have been ISR stack related.\n\nI was also a bit concerned about the inconsistency here - power_update_signals get called from both ISR and non-ISR contexts, and there is no locking or synchronisation.\nIt seems to be the whole management of the signals as a mask is a little inconsistent, since some signals don\u0027t have interrupts (and some are outputs anyway), and so there may be a difference between what the signal actually is, and what is in the mask.\n\nMaybe the mask itself should be limited to just interrupt based signals, and instead of the whole mask being recreated each time, each interrupt should just set it\u0027s own bit in an atomic.\n\nThe only places I see the mask being used are:\n - debug display of changed signals (that\u0027s not time sensitive)\n - power_wait_mask_signals_timeout\n \n The power_wait_mask_signals_timeout is the time sensitive one, and the one that relies on interrupts to work.\n \n Hmmm... perhaps we should be separating out the mask usage so that the power_wait_mask_signals_timeout function is only looking at ISR signals, and avoiding the whole \u0027power_update_signals\u0027 thing.\n What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "168a2175_627d4fa6",
      "revId": "186b7a1be94ea0d94aa586bd3eef318e8c3a8d55",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "751ba3a4_bed1a6b0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-14T03:17:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The power_wait_mask_signals_timeout is the time sensitive one, and the one that relies on interrupts to work.\n\nI don\u0027t think that\u0027s true in general; especially for power-down and S0ix (neither of which are currently implemented), the EC sometimes needs to respond quickly to signal changes from the AP.\n\n\u003e Hmmm... perhaps we should be separating out the mask usage so that the power_wait_mask_signals_timeout function is only looking at ISR signals, and avoiding the whole \u0027power_update_signals\u0027 thing.\n\nI think the key is that individual interrupts should avoid polling signals that they don\u0027t service (helping to ensure prompt processing), and both producers and consumers of the state bits should ensure they use thread-safe operations.\n\nI don\u0027t mind keeping all the signals in one value (it\u0027s convenient if there\u0027s a single SoT for every signal, and that may as well be an atomic value in RAM), but we should ensure that there are a few standard functions to safely manipulate it.",
      "parentUuid": "8d97f220_daf26a64",
      "revId": "186b7a1be94ea0d94aa586bd3eef318e8c3a8d55",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
